**New evidence**
- Direct initiation of the full-scale war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's Putin authorises 'special military operation' against Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://m.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-putin-authorises-special-military-operation-against-ukraine-2770751?ampMode=1","claim":"Putin authorized the full-scale invasion of Ukraine and said the goal was to demilitarise and 'denazify' Ukraine.","locator":"opening paragraphs and Putin quote","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report quoting the president directly on the launch decision and justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"War onset in February 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin framed the operation as defensive.","The report shows the operational choice was still an invasion."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
- Early negotiation window existed, but it was narrow and inconclusive.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Talk of compromise on both sides lifts hope for Russia-Ukraine breakthrough","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/17/uk-ukraine-crisis","claim":"Reuters reported that both Moscow and Kyiv were discussing compromise formulas, including Ukrainian neutrality outside NATO, and that proposals were nearing agreement.","locator":"lead paragraphs and quotes from Lavrov, Zelenskiy, and the Kremlin","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on the negotiations and the Russian position.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early March 2022 peace-talk window","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; shows negotiators operating under Putin's war decision and Russian state position","contrary_evidence":["Ukraine still demanded a ceasefire and withdrawal of Russian troops.","The war continued while talks remained unresolved."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}
- Russia publicly signaled a narrower war aim only after battlefield setbacks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia reframes war goals as Ukrainians advance near Kyiv","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/26/uk-ukraine-crisis","claim":"Russia signaled it was scaling back ambitions in Ukraine to focus on Donbass, saying the first phase was mostly complete.","locator":"lead and quoted statement from the Russian General Staff","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a major operational shift announced by Russian defense leadership.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late March 2022 battlefield and war-aim adjustment","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's state and military leadership","contrary_evidence":["Western officials said the original aims were a pretext for overthrowing Ukraine's government.","The shift followed military failure near Kyiv rather than a peace settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Russia made a concrete de-escalation move near Kyiv and Chernihiv after Istanbul talks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia says it will sharply cut military activity near Kyiv, Chernihiv","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/30/uk-ukraine-crisis-turkey-talks-russia","claim":"Russia said it would sharply reduce military activity around Kyiv and Chernihiv to increase mutual trust and create conditions for further negotiations.","locator":"lead paragraph and quoted statement by Deputy Defence Minister Alexander Fomin","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on an announced operational reduction tied to negotiations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Post-Istanbul talks, March 2022","ruler_attribution":"Russian state action under Putin's authority, but the reduction was tactical rather than a verified peace breakthrough","contrary_evidence":["The reduction did not end the war.","Russian forces kept major offensives elsewhere."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Early civilian harm and likely humanitarian-law violations were already widespread by late March.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Update on the human rights situation in Ukraine, 24 February – 26 March 2022","publisher":"UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-03-28","url":"https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/175994-update-human-rights-situation-ukraine-EN","claim":"The UN mission said Russian armed forces used explosive weapons with wide-area effects in populated areas and verified at least 2,909 civilian casualties by 26 March.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UN human-rights monitoring bulletin with verified casualty and conduct findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate invasion phase in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian armed forces operating under Putin's war command","contrary_evidence":["The UN noted some allegations also concerned Ukrainian forces in limited areas.","The dominant verified pattern was Russian conduct in populated areas."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Indiscriminate attacks in Kharkiv.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: 'Anyone can die at any time': Indiscriminate attacks by Russian forces in Kharkiv, Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur50/5682/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian forces launched relentless indiscriminate bombardments against Kharkiv's residential neighborhoods, killing and injuring civilians and using widely banned cluster munitions.","locator":"report summary and opening section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed field investigation with digital evidence and on-the-ground testimony.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 battlefield conduct","ruler_attribution":"Russian forces under Putin's war leadership","contrary_evidence":["Russia denied unlawful attacks.","The report found the pattern incompatible with civilian protection duties."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Mariupol theatre strike.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: Deadly Mariupol theatre strike 'a clear war crime' by Russian forces – new investigation","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/06/ukraine-deadly-mariupol-theatre-strike-a-clear-war-crime-by-russian-forces-new-investigation/","claim":"Amnesty concluded that Russian military forces likely deliberately targeted the Mariupol drama theatre despite knowing civilians were sheltering there.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigation based on survivor interviews and digital evidence, with a clear conclusion on the strike.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Spring 2022 siege warfare","ruler_attribution":"Russian military forces under Putin's command","contrary_evidence":["Russia denied bombing the theatre.","The evidence presented by Amnesty pointed the other way."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Grain exports were partly rescued by a deal, showing a limited humanitarian opening despite the war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine and Russia seal grain deal as U.S. pledges more military aid","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-07-21","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/ukraines-ports-to-reopen-under-deal-to-be-signed-friday-turkey-says-2851164","claim":"Russia and Ukraine signed a landmark deal to unblock grain exports from Black Sea ports and ease an international food crisis.","locator":"lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters wire on a major negotiated humanitarian/economic arrangement.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"July 2022 negotiated humanitarian corridor","ruler_attribution":"Russian state participation under Putin, though the arrangement was multilateral and brokered by the UN and Turkey","contrary_evidence":["The deal did not end the broader war.","Subsequent strikes threatened implementation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Putin combined mobilization with explicit nuclear coercion and annexation backing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address by the President of the Russian Federation","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://special.kremlin.ru/catalog/countries/UA/events/69390","claim":"Putin announced partial mobilization, tied the war to defense of territorial integrity, and said Russia would use all available means, adding that the warning was not bluff.","locator":"speech transcript paragraphs containing the territorial-integrity and 'not bluff' language","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Kremlin transcript of a live presidential address with direct attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Peak escalation in September 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin said the warning was defensive.","The speech escalated the war and widened coercive pressure."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.8","2B.10"]}
- The mobilization/annexation move was a major escalation, not a peace step.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia triggers plan to formally annex occupied Ukrainian regions","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/21/ukraine-crisis","claim":"Russian-controlled regions announced referendums on joining Russia after battlefield setbacks, and Reuters described the move as a serious escalation in Moscow's standoff with the West.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on the annexation trigger and escalation context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 escalation and referendum rollout","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's state and proxy apparatus in occupied Ukrainian territory","contrary_evidence":["Russia framed the move as local self-determination.","The votes were widely denounced as coerced and illegitimate."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.8","2B.10"]}
- Annexation was formalized through Russia's own legal machinery.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's annexation plan in Ukraine: Kremlin signing ceremony","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/30/ukraine-crisis-annexation","claim":"Reuters reported that Putin would host a Kremlin signing ceremony to incorporate four Ukrainian regions, a step toward formally annexing about 15 percent of Ukraine.","locator":"lead paragraphs and bullet summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters summary of the annexation legal step and Kremlin ceremony.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late September 2022 annexation","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal and state attribution to Putin's annexation decision","contrary_evidence":["Russia claimed the regions were joining by referendum.","International actors treated the annexation as illegal seizure."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.10"]}
- Russian-language independent reporting on the ratification step.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Госдума единогласно одобрила аннексию оккупированных украинских территорий","publisher":"Meduza","publication_date":"2022-10-03","url":"https://meduza.io/news/2022/10/03/gosduma-edinoglasno-odobrila-anneksiyu-okkupirovannyh-ukrainskih-territoriy","claim":"Meduza reported that Russia's State Duma ratified treaties on the 'admission' of the DNR, LNR, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson, formalizing the annexation package Putin had signed.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent Russian-language reporting on the ratification of Putin's annexation treaties.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early October 2022 formal annexation phase","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Putin's treaty-signing and Russia's state apparatus","contrary_evidence":["The article itself notes these were occupied Ukrainian territories.","The ratification did not alter international law's rejection of the annexation."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.10"]}
- Russian-language reporting from Bucha on atrocities in occupied territory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Буча ... репортаж «Медузы» из Бучи — о том, что сейчас происходит в городе, где российские солдаты убивали, насиловали и пытали жителей","publisher":"Meduza","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://meduza.io/feature/2022/10/27/boyalas-chto-povsyudu-budet-duh-smerti-dumala-zhit-zdes-ne-smogu-no-smogla","claim":"Meduza's Bucha report described Russian soldiers killing, raping, and torturing residents in a town that had been occupied by Russian forces.","locator":"headline and opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent Russian-language local reporting with direct post-occupation testimony and reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Post-withdrawal evidence on spring 2022 occupation conduct","ruler_attribution":"Attribution runs through Russian forces under Putin's invasion and occupation order","contrary_evidence":["This is journalistic reporting, not a legal judgment.","It is still strong evidence of unlawful conduct in occupied territory."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- UN human-rights office later confirmed the early invasion's civilian toll and control-linked killings.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian forces killed hundreds of civilians early in Ukraine war - UN report","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/08/ukraine-crisis-un-rights","claim":"The OHCHR report said Russian forces killed at least 441 civilians in the early days of the invasion in areas they controlled.","locator":"lead paragraph and quoted UN summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report summarizing a UN human-rights finding with direct numbers and attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective confirmation of February-April 2022 conduct","ruler_attribution":"Russian armed forces under Putin's command","contrary_evidence":["The report focused on early invaded areas, not the entire war.","Its findings still strongly corroborate unlawful early conduct."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 distinct documents across Kremlin transcripts, Reuters wires, Euronews Reuters syndications, UN/OHCHR updates, Amnesty reports, Meduza reporting, and some auxiliary diplomatic pieces.
- I rejected duplicate rewrites of the same core events, especially multiple versions of the Feb. 24 invasion address, the Sept. 21 nuclear-mobilization speech, and the annexation ceremony.
- I rejected out-of-period or drift-heavy material, including 2023-2024 retrospectives, later nuclear doctrine changes, and follow-on commentary that did not add a distinct 2022 fact.
- I rejected weak or merely derivative pieces that repeated Reuters without adding a cleaner claim or a better locator.
- I kept the set focused on distinct chapter-relevant facts: initiation of the war, the narrow peace-talk window, tactical de-escalation near Kyiv, civilian-harm evidence, grain-export diplomacy, mobilization and annexation escalation, and formal annexation ratification.

**Remaining gaps**
- The weakest area is 2B.5: direct evidence on Putin's personal control over proxies and whether he restrained them is still thinner than the evidence on invasion, annexation, and civilian harm.
- There is also limited direct evidence on private decision-making around ceasefires beyond public talk and tactical pauses.
- Better evidence on who inside the Kremlin shaped the March 2022 negotiation posture would help, but it would likely change confidence more than the overall direction of the chapter record.
- More search would probably not change the core conclusion that 2022 was an aggressive-war and annexation year; it would mostly refine attribution and confidence.
